. "$PSScriptRoot\parts.ps1"
$root = Split-Path -Parent $PSScriptRoot

$posts = @(
  @{
    Slug="how-to-hire-a-good-general-contractor-in-southwest-florida"
    Title="How to Hire a Good General Contractor in Southwest Florida"
    Date="2026-03-10"; DateDisplay="March 10, 2026"
    Img="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1541888946425-d81bb19240f5?q=80&w=1200&auto=format&fit=crop"
    Excerpt="Hiring the wrong contractor can cost you months and thousands of dollars. Here's what to actually check before you sign anything."
    Body=@"
        <p>Southwest Florida's remodeling and construction market has grown fast over the last decade, and with that growth has come a wide range of contractors - some excellent, some not licensed at all. Before you hire anyone for a kitchen remodel, addition, or storm repair, a handful of checks can save you from a project that stalls, runs over budget, or leaves work that doesn't pass inspection.</p>
        <p>The good news is that vetting a contractor doesn't take long. Most of it comes down to asking direct questions and verifying the answers instead of taking a sales pitch at face value.</p>

        <h2>What to Verify Before You Sign a Contract</h2>
        <ul class="icon-list">
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Active license and general liability insurance, confirmed directly rather than taken on their word</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> A written estimate that breaks down materials and labor, not just a single lump-sum number</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> References from recent, similar-sized projects - not just a handful of curated testimonials</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> A realistic timeline that accounts for permitting, not just labor days</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Clear communication about who's actually doing the work - their crew, or subcontractors</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Red Flags Worth Walking Away From</h3>
        <p>A contractor who pressures you to sign the same day, asks for a large upfront deposit before any work or materials are secured, or can't give you a straight answer about licensing is worth walking away from - no matter how good the price looks. In Southwest Florida specifically, be cautious of contractors who appear right after a storm offering fast repairs with no local track record; some are legitimate, but the storm-chaser pattern is common enough to warrant extra scrutiny.</p>

        <h3>Why Local Experience Matters</h3>
        <p>Florida's building code accounts for wind load, flood elevation, and coastal exposure in ways that inland states' codes don't, and a contractor with genuine Southwest Florida experience will know these requirements without having to look them up mid-project. That local knowledge is often the difference between a project that passes inspection on the first try and one that gets flagged for rework.</p>

        <h2>How to Compare Multiple Bids Fairly</h2>
        <p>It's tempting to just look at the bottom-line number on each bid and go with the lowest one, but that approach almost guarantees you're not comparing like for like. Ask every contractor you're considering the same specific questions: what materials are specified by brand and grade, not just category; what's included in site prep or demolition; and what allowance is set aside for fixtures, finishes, or unexpected conditions once work starts.</p>
        <p>A bid that comes in noticeably lower than the others usually means something is missing from the scope, not that you found a better deal. Sometimes that's a legitimate difference in materials or approach, but often it's a line item that will resurface later as a change order once the project is already underway and switching contractors is no longer realistic.</p>

        <h3>Questions Worth Asking Before You Sign</h3>
        <ul class="icon-list">
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Who is pulling the permits, and is that cost included in the bid?</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> What happens if the project runs past the estimated timeline?</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> How are change orders documented and approved?</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> What's the payment schedule, and does it align with project milestones rather than just calendar dates?</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Getting Started</h3>
        <p>None of this needs to feel adversarial - a legitimate, experienced contractor should welcome these questions rather than treat them as a challenge. If a contractor seems put off by direct questions about licensing, references, or how they document changes, that reaction alone tells you something worth knowing before you sign anything.</p>

        <h2>What to Expect Once You've Chosen a Contractor</h2>
        <p>Once you've picked someone, the relationship shouldn't just go quiet until the crew shows up. A contractor who communicates clearly before the contract is signed should continue that same level of communication once work begins - updates on scheduling, a heads-up before any change order, and a clear point of contact if something comes up mid-project.</p>
        <p>It's reasonable to ask upfront how often you'll hear from your contractor and through what channel - phone calls, texts, or a project management tool. Knowing that answer before work starts sets expectations on both sides and avoids the frustration of feeling like you have to chase down updates on your own project.</p>
"@
  },
  @{
    Slug="avoid-these-3-things-when-hiring-someone-to-build-your-home"
    Title="Avoid These 3 Things When Hiring Someone to Build Your Home"
    Date="2026-02-18"; DateDisplay="February 18, 2026"
    Img="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1489599849927-2ee91cede3ba?q=80&w=1200&auto=format&fit=crop"
    Excerpt="Building a new home is a major investment. These three mistakes are the ones we see most often - and they're all avoidable."
    Body=@"
        <p>Building a new home is one of the largest financial decisions most homeowners make, and it's also one where a handful of avoidable mistakes tend to cause the most expensive problems. After years of new construction projects across North Port and Southwest Florida, three issues come up more than any others.</p>

        <h2>Mistake #1: Choosing Price Over Track Record</h2>
        <p>The lowest bid on a new construction project is rarely the best value once you account for change orders, delays, and rework. A contractor who quotes significantly below the rest of the field is often missing scope somewhere in the estimate - and that missing scope shows up later as an unplanned cost. Comparing detailed, itemized bids rather than bottom-line numbers makes it much easier to spot this before you sign.</p>
        <p>It's worth asking every bidder the same specific questions - what's included in site prep, what allowance is set for fixtures and finishes, and what happens if permitting takes longer than expected - so you're comparing apples to apples rather than just a final number.</p>

        <h3>Mistake #2: Skipping the Permitting Conversation</h3>
        <ul class="icon-list">
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Confirm who's pulling the permits, and when</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Ask which county or city jurisdiction applies to your lot</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Confirm flood elevation requirements are already factored into the design</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Skipping this conversation is one of the most common reasons new construction projects stall midway - a design that looks great on paper can hit a permitting snag that adds weeks or months if it wasn't checked upfront.</p>

        <h3>Mistake #3: Not Getting Everything in Writing</h3>
        <p>Verbal promises about upgrades, timelines, or included finishes don't hold up when a dispute comes up later. Every material change, timeline shift, or scope addition should be documented in writing before work proceeds - not because you expect a problem, but because a written record protects both sides if memories differ six months into a project.</p>

        <h2>A Fourth Mistake Worth Mentioning: Underestimating Site Conditions</h2>
        <p>Southwest Florida's sandy soil, high water table, and flood elevation requirements mean site conditions can add cost and time to a new build in ways that aren't always obvious from a standard bid. Soil compaction testing, fill requirements, and drainage planning are all site-specific, and a contractor who hasn't walked your particular lot in detail before bidding is more likely to hit a costly surprise once site work actually begins.</p>
        <p>Ask any contractor you're considering how they've handled similar soil or elevation conditions on other builds in your area, and ask to see an example. A vague answer here is often a sign the site work portion of the estimate is more of a placeholder than a real number.</p>

        <h3>What a Thorough Site Assessment Looks Like</h3>
        <ul class="icon-list">
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> A soil test or compaction assessment specific to your lot</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> A clear answer on flood elevation requirements before the foundation is designed</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> A drainage plan that accounts for Florida's heavy seasonal rain, not just code minimums</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Putting It All Together</h3>
        <p>None of these four mistakes are complicated to avoid once you know to look for them. Comparing bids on equal footing, asking direct permitting questions, documenting everything in writing, and confirming a contractor has actually assessed your site conditions - together, these steps cover most of what separates a smooth new construction project from a stressful one.</p>

        <h2>Building a Realistic Budget From the Start</h2>
        <p>Beyond the mistakes above, one of the most common sources of frustration in new construction is a budget that was never realistic to begin with. It's tempting to anchor on the lowest number you've heard, even informally, and treat everything above that as an overrun rather than an accurate reflection of the project's real cost.</p>
        <p>Building in a contingency - typically 10 to 15 percent of the total budget - for unforeseen conditions or changes you decide to make along the way gives you room to adjust without feeling like every change is blowing the budget. A contractor who encourages this kind of buffer from the start is generally being more honest with you than one who presents an all-in number with no room for the unexpected.</p>
"@
  },
  @{
    Slug="5-structural-features-every-florida-kitchen-should-have"
    Title="5 Structural Features Every Florida Kitchen Should Have"
    Date="2026-01-22"; DateDisplay="January 22, 2026"
    Img="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1556909212-d5b604d0c90d?q=80&w=1200&auto=format&fit=crop"
    Excerpt="Florida's humidity and storm exposure call for kitchen materials and layouts that differ from a standard remodel. Here's what to prioritize."
    Body=@"
        <p>A kitchen remodel in Southwest Florida faces conditions that a lot of national design trends don't account for - year-round humidity, storm exposure, and in many homes, an open layout tied directly into hurricane-rated windows and doors. Building for those conditions from the start avoids problems that show up months after a standard remodel is finished.</p>

        <h2>What to Prioritize in a Florida Kitchen Remodel</h2>
        <p>Material selection matters more here than in a drier climate. Solid wood cabinetry that isn't properly sealed can warp with humidity swings over a few years, while moisture-resistant engineered materials and marine-grade finishes hold up significantly longer in a Gulf Coast home.</p>

        <h3>Five Features Worth Building In</h3>
        <ul class="icon-list">
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Moisture-resistant cabinet materials rated for high-humidity environments</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Proper exhaust ventilation sized for Florida's humidity load, not just code minimum</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Impact-rated windows if the kitchen has exterior glazing</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Non-porous countertop materials that resist staining and bacterial growth in humid conditions</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> GFCI-protected outlets placed with Florida's power-surge frequency from summer storms in mind</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Open Layouts and Storm Considerations</h3>
        <p>Many Florida homes favor open kitchen layouts that connect directly to a lanai or great room, which is great for everyday living but worth planning around if that opening includes sliding glass doors - those should carry the same impact rating as the rest of the home's storm protection, not be treated as a separate, lower-priority item during a kitchen remodel.</p>

        <h2>Ventilation and Indoor Air Quality</h2>
        <p>Florida's humidity doesn't just threaten cabinetry and countertops - it also affects indoor air quality in ways that show up as lingering cooking odors, condensation on cabinet interiors, or a general stuffiness that no amount of air freshener actually solves. A properly sized exhaust hood, vented to the exterior rather than simply recirculating air through a filter, makes a bigger difference in a Florida kitchen than it typically would in a drier climate.</p>
        <p>Undersized or poorly placed range hoods are one of the more common oversights we see in kitchens that were renovated without a Florida-specific approach. It's an easy detail to overlook during design, since it doesn't show up as a problem until months after the project is finished and the humidity has had time to work its way into the cabinetry.</p>

        <h3>Lighting Choices That Hold Up to Humidity</h3>
        <ul class="icon-list">
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Sealed, moisture-rated fixtures for any lighting near the sink or cooktop</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> LED fixtures, which run cooler and add less heat load to an already warm climate</li>
          <li><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5"/></svg> Corrosion-resistant hardware for any fixture near an exterior-facing window or door</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Working With a Contractor Who Builds for Florida Conditions</h3>
        <p>A lot of kitchen design inspiration - from magazines, social media, or national home shows - comes from climates that simply don't deal with the same humidity and storm exposure Southwest Florida homes face. That's not a reason to avoid a particular style or trend, but it is a reason to work with a contractor who will flag when a specific material or design choice needs a Florida-specific adjustment before it becomes a problem a few years down the line.</p>

        <h2>Electrical Capacity for a Modern Kitchen</h2>
        <p>Older Florida homes were often wired for a much lighter kitchen load than what a modern remodel actually needs - a full suite of appliances, under-cabinet lighting, and dedicated circuits for things like a wine fridge or a second oven add up quickly. Before finalizing a kitchen layout, it's worth having the existing electrical panel assessed to confirm it can support the finished design without being maxed out on day one.</p>
        <p>This is easy to overlook during the design phase, since electrical capacity isn't something most homeowners think about until an inspector flags it or a breaker starts tripping after move-in. Addressing it as part of the initial remodel, rather than as a separate project later, is almost always the more cost-effective approach.</p>

        <h3>A Final Word on Planning Ahead</h3>
        <p>Every one of these features - moisture-resistant materials, proper ventilation, impact-rated glazing, non-porous countertops, adequate electrical capacity - costs more to build in upfront than it does to retrofit after the fact, and it's far less disruptive than tackling each issue separately as it surfaces over the years. A kitchen designed with Florida's actual climate in mind from day one tends to hold up better and need less rework than one designed around a generic national template.</p>
"@
  }
)

foreach ($p in $posts) {
    $body = @"
